{"id": "490", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "gt": "Based on the given passages, you can track who liked your tweet by using websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm. Additionally, you can set up your Twitter account to receive email notifications whenever someone marks one of your tweets as their favorite.", "pred": "To tell who liked your tweet, you need to check the 'likes' section on your tweet. This section typically displays the usernames of the individuals who have liked your tweet.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Answer: To tell who liked your tweet, you need to check the 'likes' section on your tweet. This section typically displays the usernames of the individuals who have liked your tweet.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nTo determine who liked your tweet, you need to check the 'likes' section on your tweet. This section typically displays the usernames of the individuals who have liked your tweet.\nThe next logical step would be to explain how to access the 'likes' section. This could involve providing instructions on how to navigate to the tweet you are referring to and then find the section where the likes are displayed.\nThe final step would be to describe the process of accessing the 'likes' section in detail. This might include mentioning any specific icons or buttons related to the 'likes' section, and how to interact with them to view the list of likes.\n\nAnswer: To tell who liked your tweet, you need to check the 'likes' section on your tweet. This section typically displays the usernames of the individuals who have liked your tweet.", "time_sec": 42.597, "generation_time_seconds": 30.578, "selection_time_seconds": 12.0188, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [2610, 1184, 311, 728, 1526, 279, 11809, 594, 16230, 821, 11, 1741, 438, 13151, 11, 2112, 25415, 11, 323, 6042, 13, 151645], "logp