{"id": "519", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how raffles work:\n\n1. Reverse Raffle: In this type of raffle, the numbers on the entry tickets are drawn in reverse order, starting with the last number and ending with the first number. For example, if there are 55 entries, the last number drawn wins the grand prize, followed by the next-to-last number, and so on until the first number. Each entry ticket costs $50, and a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket worth $1,000 and two $500 prizes (for the early bird drawing and the first ticket pulled).\n2. 50-50 Draw: This is a raffle held during a single day at a specific event. Tickets are sold to patrons throughout the event by roving staff with handheld electronic devices. The gross proceeds of 50-50 tickets sold at the event go into a prize pool, and 50% of the prize pool is awarded to the winner of a random draw that takes place at the end of the event. The remaining 50% goes to a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\n3. Additional Raffles: In addition to the main raffle, there may be additional raffles for drinks and dinner. These raffles are not included in the original ticket price and require separate entry fees. One of these raffles offers the chance to win $10,000!\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nExamine the passage for details on how raffles work, including steps such as ticket purchase, drawing of winning tickets, and distribution of prizes.\nLook for any specific examples, numbers, or comparisons in the passage that describe how raffles operate.\nIdentify any relevant information about the rules, process, or elements of a raffle as described in the passage.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 41.32, "generation_time_seconds": 24.2876, "selection_time_seconds": 17.032, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [17075, 311, 4226, 3118, 389, 2661, 46769, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-3.323, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.007, -0.0], "cumulative_logprob": -3.3308, "norm_logp": -0.3701, "input_len": 137, "output_tokens": 9}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 1376, 5424, 315, 279, 3405, 25, 576, 3405, 17064, 911, 279, 1882, 476, 29026, 315, 1246, 435, 80535, 975, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.9837, -0.0016, -0.1784, -0.2522, -1.0918, -0.3649, -1.2592, -0.2327, -0.2513, -0.7747, -0.0844, -0.1864, -0.1856, -1.0397, -2.5958, -0.3933, -1.1963, -0.0007, -0.0685, -0.0098, -0.0, -0.0054, -0.1466, -0.3436], "cumulative_logprob": -12.6466, "norm_logp": -0.5269, "input_len": 137, "output_tokens": 24}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [840, 19991, 279, 21085, 369, 3565, 389, 1246, 435, 80535, 975, 11, 2670, 7354, 1741, 438, 11727, 7627, 11, 13330, 315, 10987, 14403, 11, 323, 7982, 315, 37408, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.3587, -0.2522, -0.0142, -3.4459, -0.149, -2.7521, -0.6278, -0.4172, -0.0611, -0.0001, -1.1999, -0.6435, -0.7631, -5.5321, -3.5398, -0.0, -0.0719, -0.6667, -0.0027, -0.072, -2.6184, -2.2092, -0.1607, -0.0003, -0.1107, -2.1262, -0.0006, -0.0189, -0.0026, -0.1254], "cumulative_logprob": -28.943, "norm_logp": -0.9648, "input_len": 137, "output_tokens": 30}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28959, 279, 9760, 1995, 504, 279, 2661, 21085, 911, 1246, 435, 80535, 975, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-5.0194, -1.1691, -1.2723, -0.2649, -0.168, -0.0003, -0.7725, -0.5384, -0.5449, -0.0759, -0.0083, -0.0, -0.0072, -0.0524, -0.0133], "cumulative_logprob": -9.9069, "norm_logp": -0.6605, "input_len": 137, "output_tokens": 15}, {"phase": "vote", "beam": null, "token_ids": [24728, 220, 18, 25, 1374, 19991, 279, 21085, 369, 356