{"id": "57", "question": "how can i keep birds from building a nest on my porch", "gt": "To prevent birds from building a nest on your porch, you can attach bird netting to the eves and top of your porch with staples. Additionally, you can install bird netting over your light fixture to deter birds from accessing it. Another option is to mount a plastic hawk on or near your porch to scare the birds away. It's important to note that any product used to deter birds should be nonflammable and safe to use.", "pred": "By taking these steps, you can create an environment that is less appealing to birds and reduce the likelihood of them building a nest on your porch.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "By taking these steps, you can create an environment that is less appealing to birds and reduce the likelihood of them building a nest on your porch.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nAnalyzing the physical details of the porch to understand the environment where birds might build nests.\nObserving known bird habits and preferences for nesting locations.\nAnalyzing the physical details of the porch to understand the environment where birds might build nests.\n\nAnswer: To keep birds from building a nest on your porch, you can take several steps based on the physical details of the porch and bird habits. First, consider the materials and structure of the porch. If it has any crevices or areas where birds can perch, filling these gaps with weatherproof materials can prevent birds from accessing the space. For example, using wire mesh or plastic caps to cover any openings can be effective.\n\nSecond, consider the lighting and temperature of the porch. Birds are less likely to nest in areas that are too hot or too cold. If your porch is exposed to direct sunlight, providing shade or using reflective materials can help regulate the temperature. Similarly, if the porch is too cold, adding insulation or using a heat lamp can make it less appealing for nesting.\n\nThird, consider the presence of food sources. Birds are attracted to areas where they can find food. If your porch has any food sources, such as bird feeders or pet food, consider moving these items away from the porch or covering them to prevent birds from accessing them.\n\nFinally, consider the presence of predators. Birds are less likely to nest in areas where they feel threatened. If your porch is located near areas with predators, such as cats or dogs, consider installing a barrier or fence to protect the birds.\n\nBy taking these steps, you can create an environment that is less appealing to birds and reduce the likelihood of them building a nest on your porch.", "time_sec": 60.552, "generation_time_seconds": 42.4119, "selection_time_seconds": 18.14, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [73307, 20371, 279, 6961, 3565, 315, 279, 44647, 311, 3535, 279, 4573, 1380, 19654, 2578, 1936, 95121, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-6.3915, -0.0666, -0.0625, -0.4061, -0.0395, -1.4307, -0.0449, -0.0612, -0.7294, -1.7368, -2.7674, -1.7391, -0.1994, -0.2297, -0.1574, -0.5364, -0.1398, -0.0775, -0.0314], "cumulative_logprob": -16.8473, "norm_logp": -0.8867, "input_len": 144, "output_tokens": 19}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 3151, 5671, 389, 279, 44647, 1380, 19654, 2578, 1936, 95121, 323, 2908, 1667, 6961, 29640, 476, 82620, 82, 311, 5358, 66710, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.8825, -0.0056, -0.6117, -0.72, -2.9096, -0.5929, -0.0789, -0.0, -0.0402, -0.0572, -0.796, -0.4114, -0.0561, -3.6567, -0.2026, -1.3473, -0.3141, -0.0125, -0.7506, -0.0143, -0.0005, -1.0849, -0.0517, -0.1727, -0.2268, -0.0122], "cumulative_logprob": -16.009, "norm_logp": -0.6157, "input_len": 144, "output_tokens": 26}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [1249, 3535, 1246, 311, 5358, 19654, 504, 4752, 95121, 389, 264, 44647, 11, 432, 594, 7565, 311, 2908,