07, -0.341, -0.0508, -0.4803, -0.0088, -0.1829, -1.3745, -0.0, -0.4582, -1.9624, -0.0002, -0.0, -1.6772, -0.142, -0.0102, -0.128, -0.2978, -1.0783, -0.0248, -0.0003, -0.3472, -0.1015, -1.8458, -0.2345, -0.4793, -0.0451, -0.0094, -1.3844, -0.6559, -0.9394, -0.5075, -1.228, -2.1231, -2.4994, -0.2436, -1.2611, -0.4367, -2.0293, -0.0464, -0.0001, -1.1132, -0.3157, -2.8435, -0.1814, -0.0, -0.0, -0.095, -0.0001, -0.2521, -0.0], "cumulative_logprob": -35.1143, "norm_logp": -0.6054, "input_len": 452, "output_tokens": 58}, {"phase": "final", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [17075, 311, 4226, 3118, 389, 2661, 46769, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.5204, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0022, -0.0001, -0.0289, -0.0004], "cumulative_logprob": -0.5525, "norm_logp": -0.0614, "input_len": 181, "output_tokens": 9}], "output_tokens": 446, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "231", "question": "oil stain on concrete driveway", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is the answer to the question \"How do you remove oil stains from a concrete driveway?\"\n\nTo remove oil stains from a concrete driveway, you can use one of the following methods:\n\n1. Oven cleaner, goop hand cleaner, and carburetor cleaner (as mentioned in Passage 1).\n2. A commercial-grade cleaner applied with a brush or broom, followed by scrubbing with a pressure washer (as described in Passage 3).\n\nBefore starting the cleaning process, it is recommended to wash away any dirt or debris from the affected area (Passage 2). Additionally, it is important to avoid using high-pressure hoses to clean the stain, as this can push the oil deeper into the pavement (Passage 2).\n\nIt is also noted that the size of the oil spill and the length of time the stain has been present will affect the method of removal (Passage 3). For small and stubborn stains, the poultice method may be effective (Passage 2).", "pred": "To remove an oil stain from a concrete driveway, you can use a mixture of baking soda and water. First, sprinkle baking soda over the oil stain to absorb it. Let it sit for about 30 minutes. Then, rinse the area with water to remove the baking soda and the absorbed oil. For stubborn stains, you can use a solution of dish soap and water. Apply the solution to the stain and scrub gently with a brush. Rinse the area thoroughly with water. If the stain persists, you may need to use a commercial oil stain remover. Always test any cleaning solution on a small, inconspicuous area first to ensure it does not damage the concrete.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Answer: To remove an oil stain from a concrete driveway, you can use a mixture of baking soda and water. First, sprinkle baking soda over the oil stain to absorb it. Let it sit for about 30 minutes. Then, rinse the area with water to remove the baking soda and the absorbed oil. For stubborn stains, you can use a solution of dish soap and water. Apply the solution to the stain and scrub gently with a brush. Rinse the area thoroughly with water. If the stain persists, you may need to use a commercial oil stain remover. Always test any cleaning solution on a small, inconspicuous area first to ensure it does not damage the concrete.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nUnderstand the context: The question is about removing an oil stain from a concrete driveway. This involves analyzing the characteristics and properties of both oil and concrete.\nIdentify the properties of oil: Oil is typically a liquid that is hydrophobic, meaning it does not mix well with water. It can also form a semi-permanent film on surfaces, including concrete.\nIdentify the properties of concrete: Concrete is a porous material, which means it can absorb liquids, including oil. The porosity of concrete can vary depending on the type of aggregate and cement used in its manufacture. Some types of concrete may have more open pores than others, which affects how oil interacts with the surface.\n\nAnswer: To remove an oil stain from a concrete driveway, you can use a mixture of baking soda and water. First, sprinkle baking soda over the oil stain to absorb it. Let it sit for about 30 minutes. Then, rinse the area with water to remove the baking soda and the absorbed oil. For stubborn stains, you can use a solution of dish soap and water. Apply the solution to the stain and scrub gently with a brush. Rinse the area thoroughly with water. If the stain persists, you may need to use a commercial oil stain remover. Always test any cleaning solution on a small, inconspicuous area first to ensure it does not damage the concrete.", "time_sec": 65.812, "generation_time_seconds": 43.9332, "selection_time_seconds": 21.8787, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 3151, 1714, 