{"id": "298", "question": "how to make hot water heater circulate", "gt": "To make a hot water heater circulate, a hot water circulation pump is needed. This pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line. The pump circulates this water through the water heater as needed to keep it hot, ensuring hot water is always available. Alternatively, recirculating pumps can be used to rapidly pull hot water from a water heater, while simultaneously sending cool water from the hot water lines back to the water heater to be reheated and reused. These systems can be controlled by the push of a button, a timer, or a motion sensor. However, it should be noted that heat loss from the pipes can occur, requiring the water heater to use more fuel.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nTo make a hot water heater circulate, you can use the following steps:\nIdentify the type of hot water heater you have, as different types have different methods for circulation. For example, if you have a gravity-fed water heater, make sure the hot water tank is positioned above the cold water tank to allow gravity to pull the water through the system. If you have a tankless water heater, circulation is typically built into the system and may not require manual intervention.\nExplain how to check and replace any necessary components, such as check valves or circulation pumps, if you encounter issues with the circulation of your hot water heater.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 77.911, "generation_time_seconds": 42.0276, "selection_time_seconds": 35.8836, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [785, 3405, 17064, 911, 1246, 311, 1281, 264, 4017, 3015, 42238, 4225, 6334, 13, 2014, 4226, 419, 1