, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.9108, -0.1951, -0.0, -0.0, -0.3537, -0.7473, -0.2199, -0.0016], "cumulative_logprob": -2.4284, "norm_logp": -0.3035, "input_len": 443, "output_tokens": 8}, {"phase": "final", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [17075, 311, 4226, 3118, 389, 2661, 46769, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.3885, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0025, -0.0001, -0.0301, -0.0007], "cumulative_logprob": -0.4227, "norm_logp": -0.047, "input_len": 189, "output_tokens": 9}], "output_tokens": 873, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 4.4, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "632", "question": "similarity and difference between nervous system and endocrine system", "gt": "The nervous system and endocrine system are both critical to maintaining homeostasis in the body by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems. They both use chemical messengers, although their method of transmission and release differs. The nervous system is made of interconnected neuron cells and uses electrochemical pulses for signal transmission. In contrast, the endocrine system is made of discrete glands that use hormones for signal transmission and utilizes the circulatory system to carry these signals.", "pred": "In summary, the nervous system and endocrine system are both essential for the body's functioning, but they operate in different ways and have distinct roles. The nervous system transmits information through electrical and chemical signals, while the endocrine system produces and releases hormones. Both systems work together to maintain homeostasis.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "In summary, the nervous system and endocrine system are both essential for the body's functioning, but they operate in different ways and have distinct roles. The nervous system transmits information through electrical and chemical signals, while the endocrine system produces and releases hormones. Both systems work together to maintain homeostasis.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nCompare the nervous system and endocrine system.\nIdentify common functions and characteristics of both the nervous system and endocrine system.\nIdentify distinct functions of the nervous system and endocrine system, highlighting how they interact with each other.\n\nThe nervous system and endocrine system are both crucial for the body's functioning, but they operate in different ways and have distinct roles.\n\nThe nervous system is responsible for transmitting information throughout the body. It consists of the brain, spinal cord, and nerves. The nervous system uses electrical and chemical signals to communicate. For example, when you touch a hot object, the sensory neurons in your skin send a signal to the spinal cord, which then sends a signal to the muscles to move your hand away from the heat. This process is known as a reflex.\n\nThe endocrine system, on the other hand, is responsible for producing and releasing hormones. Hormones are chemical messengers that travel through the bloodstream to target organs and tissues. For example, the thyroid gland produces thyroid hormones, which regulate metabolism. The pancreas produces insulin, which regulates blood sugar levels.\n\nBoth the nervous system and endocrine system work together to maintain homeostasis, or a stable internal environment. For example, when you exercise, your body temperature increases. The nervous system sends signals to the sweat glands to produce sweat, which helps to cool you down. At the same time, the endocrine system releases hormones that help to regulate your heart rate and blood pressure.\n\nIn summary, the nervous system and endocrine system are both essential for the body's functioning, but they operate in different ways and have distinct roles. The nervous system transmits information through electrical and chemical signals, while the endocrine system produces and releases hormones. Both systems work together to maintain homeostasis.", "time_sec": 49.898, "generation_time_seconds": 30.1308, "selection_time_seconds": 19.7668, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [34402, 1361, 1948, 279, 22596, 1849, 323, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 382], "logp_list": [-4.0171, -0.6945, -0.3567, -0.1349, -0.0004, -0.0558, -0.0, -0.4608, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0, -7.1992], "cumulative_logprob": -12.9194, "norm_logp": -1.0766, "input_len": 142, "output_tokens": 12}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [20629, 22596