{"id": "429", "question": "how do twins happen", "gt": "Twins can happen in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins occur when one zygote splits into two embryos, while dizygotic (fraternal) twins happen when two different eggs are fertilized by two separate sperm cells. The likelihood of dizygotic twins is influenced by factors such as West African descent, being between 30 and 40 years old, being taller and heavier than average, and having had several previous pregnancies. Conjoined twins occur when the division of the zygote happens within 8 days of fertilization and can result in complications due to shared organs.", "pred": "The occurrence of twins can be influenced by various factors, including maternal age, genetic predisposition, and certain medical conditions. For example, women over the age of 35 are more likely to have twins due to the increased likelihood of ovulating multiple eggs. Additionally, some genetic disorders can increase the chances of having twins.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "The occurrence of twins can be influenced by various factors, including maternal age, genetic predisposition, and certain medical conditions. For example, women over the age of 35 are more likely to have twins due to the increased likelihood of ovulating multiple eggs. Additionally, some genetic disorders can increase the chances of having twins.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nExamine the specific biological processes involved in the development of twins.\nIdentify common types of twins (monozygotic and dizygotic) and explain how each type forms.\nAnalyze the genetic and environmental factors that contribute to the occurrence of twins.\n\nTwins can occur through two main types: monozygotic and dizygotic twins.\n\n1. Monozygotic twins: These twins develop from a single fertilized egg that splits into two embryos. This process is called cleavage. The resulting twins share the same genetic material and are identical in appearance. Monozygotic twins are relatively rare, occurring in about 1 in 250 pregnancies.\n\n2. Dizygotic twins: These twins develop from two separate eggs that are fertilized by two different sperm cells. This process is called fertilization. The resulting twins have different genetic material and are not identical in appearance. Dizygotic twins are more common, occurring in about 1 in 70 pregnancies.\n\nThe occurrence of twins can be influenced by various factors, including maternal age, genetic predisposition, and certain medical conditions. For example, women over the age of 35 are more likely to have twins due to the increased likelihood of ovulating multiple eggs. Additionally, some genetic disorders can increase the chances of having twins.", "time_sec": 47.147, "generation_time_seconds": 31.4998, "selection_time_seconds": 15.6473, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [785, 32711, 7354, 525, 24391], "logp_list": [-1.8484, -1.2608, -0.0417, -0.0575, -1.6865], "cumulative_logprob": -4.8949, "norm_logp": -0.979, "input_len": 136, "output_tokens": 5}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 1376, 3565, 504, 279, 3405, 25, 576, 3405, 17064, 911, 279, 16953, 315, 43636, 18003, 323, 24297, 264, 2033, 3118, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 13, 576, 4647, 364, 15560, 1330, 6, 646, 8300, 311, 2987, 364, 1713, 938, 43636, 6, 476, 364, 1626, 266, 2978, 43636, 2894, 323, 279, 21085, 1265, 3410, 1995, 389, 1246, 1817, 943, 7586, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.3127, -0.0028, -0.1638, -1.1929, -1.5663, -2.455, -0.0001, -1.4194, -0.4074, -0.1465, -0.0374, -0.2578, -0.0899, -0.1864, -1.4523, -0.3014, -0.7965, -3.5496, -4.9106, -2.3832, -0.5589, -1.9649, -0.1013, -0.0114, -0.0657, -0.359, -0.0575, -0.0291, -4.1344, -2.1598, -0.9969, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0001, -2.6023, -0.0525, -0.0013, -0.8941, -4.7166, -1.2319, -0.0, -1.113, -0.0081, -0.1857, -0.0, -0.0015, -0.0001, 0.0, -0.0001