, 821, 27875, 702, 3635, 803, 45841, 11, 27362, 9675, 311, 1281, 821, 31405, 11181, 323, 7269, 24586, 15024, 13, 4220, 29016, 82687, 614, 537, 1172, 5497, 279, 1616, 975, 374, 2814, 714, 1083, 279, 12783, 323, 27323, 315, 8256, 11, 448, 264, 7046, 24654, 389, 7377, 51982, 323, 279, 5726, 311, 975, 11182, 7887, 304, 264, 4108, 4573, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.0809, -0.0513, -0.5506, -0.0023, -0.3898, -0.1443, -0.0128, -0.0084, -0.8496, -0.9234, -0.0325, -0.2112, -0.9693, -0.144, -0.001, -0.3378, -0.1949, -0.0036, -0.0613, -0.8678, -0.0, -0.0874, -0.351, -0.3462, -0.4146, -0.6178, -0.3811, -0.444, -0.1706, -0.1671, -0.2311, -0.0208, -0.8983, -0.7022, -0.0344, -0.0675, -0.0119, -0.1611, -0.0439, -0.788, -0.0013, -0.0071, -0.6892, -0.6904, -1.1737, -0.4076, -0.7969, -0.2444, -0.0474, -0.004, -0.069, -0.0122, -0.7162, -0.2382, -0.1113, -0.1094, -0.2716, -0.1491, -0.6434, -0.9392, -0.0, -0.9177, -0.0026, -0.0006, -0.0022, -0.087, -0.5475, -0.1886, -0.4154, -0.1742, -0.8539, -0.0, -0.1157, -0.0133, -0.0, -0.0043, -0.0, -0.0407, -0.9727, -0.1674, -0.0517, -0.0392, -0.0506, -1.0399, -0.0001, -0.058, -0.4234, -0.0013, -0.0001, -0.2073, -0.4569, -1.0438, -0.7032, -0.0389, -0.5353, -0.1815, -0.0367, -0.0287, -0.3789, -0.0, -0.8053, -0.1893, -0.2463, -0.0446, -0.002, -0.1043, -0.0056, -0.252, -1.0465, -0.787, -0.5488, -0.4503, -0.092, -0.0444, -0.963, -1.774, -0.7376, -0.2237, -0.0855, -0.0016, -0.3178, -0.4797, -0.0115, -1.2374, -0.0048, -0.0, -0.6327, -0.6245, -0.0001, -0.3824, -0.5845, -0.1772, -0.0213, -0.0112, -0.0215], "cumulative_logprob": -42.7871, "norm_logp": -0.3169, "input_len": 195, "output_tokens": 135}], "output_tokens": 918, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 23.94, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "633", "question": "similarity and difference between nervous system and endocrine system", "gt": "The nervous system and endocrine system have both similarities and differences. \n\nSimilarities:\n1. Both systems are dedicated to maintaining homeostasis and coordinating the activities of cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\n2. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\n3. Chemical messengers play a crucial role in both systems.\n\nDifferences:\n1. The nervous system is composed of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made up of glands.\n2. Transmission in the nervous system occurs through electrochemical pulses, while chemical hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the endocrine system.\n3. The nervous system utilizes interconnected cells to transmit signals, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected.\n4. The nervous system transmits signals through neurons, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.", "pred": "The nervous system and the endocrine system are both involved in the body's regulation of functions, but they differ in their means of communication and the speed of their responses. The nervous system uses electrical signals, known as action potentials, which are transmitted along neurons through synapses, while the endocrine system uses hormones, which are chemical messengers that are released into the bloodstream and travel to target cells to regulate physiological processes. The nervous system can transmit signals very quickly, with action potentials traveling at speeds of up to 120 meters per second, whereas the endocrine system has a slower response time because hormones must be produced and released into the bloodstream, which can take several minutes or even longer.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Answer: The nervous system and the endocrine system are both involved in the body's regulation of functions, but they differ in their means of communication and the speed of their responses. The nervous system uses electrical signals, known as action potentials, which are transmitted along neurons through synapses, while the endocrine system uses hormones, which are chemical messengers that are released into the bloodstream and travel to target cells to regulate physiological processes. The nervous system can transmit signals very quickly, with action potentials traveling at speeds of up to 120 meters per second, whereas the endocrine system has a slower response time because hormones must be produced and released into the bloodstream, which can take several minutes or even longer.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nThe reasoning steps are:\n1. **Identify the systems**: The nervous system and the endocrine system are both involved in the body's regulation of functions. The nervous system consists of neurons that transmit signals via electrical and chemical means, while the endocrine system uses hormones to regulate various functions.\n2. **Compare the means of communication**: \n   - The nervous system uses electrical signals, known as action potentials, which are transmitted along neurons through synapses.\n   - The endocrine system uses hormones, which are chemical messengers that are released into the bloodstream and travel to target cells to regulate physiological processes.\n3. **Compare the speed of communication**:\n   - The nervous system can transmit signals very quickly, with action potentials traveling at speeds of up to 120 meters per second.\n   - The endocrine system has a slower response time because hormones must be produced and released into the bloodstream, which can take several minutes or even longer.\n\nAnswer: The nervous system and the endocrine system are both involved in the body's regulation of functions, but they differ in their means of communication and the speed of their responses. The nervous system uses electrical signals, known as action potentials, which are transmitted along neurons through synapses, while the endocrine system uses hormones, which are chemical messengers that are released into the bloodstream and travel to target cells to regulate physiological processes. The nervous system can transmit signals very quickly, with action potentials traveling at speeds of up to 120 meters per second, whereas the endocrine system has a slower response time because hormones must be produced and released into the bloodstream, which can take several minutes or even longer.", "time_sec": 76.107, "generation_time_seconds": 46.3221, "selection_time_seconds": 29.7847, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [785, 32711, 7354, 525, 510, 16, 13, 3070, 28301, 1437, 279, 5942, 95518, 576, 22596, 1849, 323, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 525, 2176, 6398, 304, 279, 2487, 594, 19256, 315, 5746, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 17167, 315, 33213, 429, 29282, 17289, 4566, 19734, 323, 11483, 3363, 11, 1393, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 5711, 43215, 311, 36277, 5257, 5746, 382], "logp_list": [-2.0528, -1.2175, -0.0351, -0.0713, -1.6815, -0.0185, -0.0012, -4.0287, -0.1296, -0.0016, -0.0392, -1.3808, -0.0991, -0.4111, -0.7529, -0.0163, -0.035, -0.2148, 0.0, 0.0, -0.0, -0.0134, -0.9215, -1.4125, -0.0, -1.2287, -0.3237, -0.0019, -1.8377, -3.1336, -0.4738, -1.5817, -0.0962, -0.0014, -0.0, -2.3468, -0.0006, -2.3115, -0.6309, -0.0157, -0.4867, -2.6603, -0.0598, -0.6157, -0.0001, -0.5052, -0.0998, -0.0735, -0.0, 0.0, 0.0, -0.0, -1.4595, -0.0242, -0.1712, -0.0735, -0.6267, -1.998, -1.0672], "cumulative_logprob": -38.44, "norm_logp": -0.6515, "input_len": 142, "output_tokens": 59}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [34402, 1361, 1948, 22596, 1849, 323, 835, 77638, 1849, 2924, 2176, 5942, 1486, 264, 3476, 304, 57399, 5257, 47031, 5746, 13, 11733, 5942, 19032, 448, 7761, 304, 279, 2487, 11, 7892, 807, 653, 773, 304, 2155, 5510, 13, 151645], 