{"id": "670", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each item of inventory when it is sold. This is calculated by taking the average cost of all inventories currently available. This cost fluctuates as inventory prices change. The weighted average cost per unit is determined by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale. This cost is then assigned to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. This method is often used when it's impossible to assign specific costs to individual units or when the accounting system isn't sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. The recorded amount of inventory on hand using this method represents a value between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nExamine the provided passages for specific information on the weighted average inventory costing method. Identify any examples, numbers, or comparisons that explain how it is calculated.\nLook for any examples or detailed explanations within the passages that illustrate the process of calculating the weighted average cost per unit. If there are specific calculations or formulas, note them down.\nAnalyze the provided passages for a comprehensive understanding of the weighted average inventory costing method, including any relevant examples, numbers, or comparisons. Identify the formula or methodology used to calculate the weighted average cost per unit. Note any limitations, assumptions, or conditions mentioned in the calculations.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 57.79, "generation_time_seconds": 29.6738, "selection_time_seconds": 28.1159, "recor