{"id": "209", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Based on the given passages, chard is a type of vegetable that belongs to the same subspecies as beetroot and is characterized by its large, fleshy leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It has a stronger, more assertive flavor than spinach and can be used raw in salads or cooked like in pizzoccheri. Chard is a beet that has been selected for leaf production rather than storage root formation and can be harvested when young and tender or after maturity. It is an attractive ornamental that adds color to the vegetable garden, but it is extremely perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator for a maximum of 2-3 days.", "pred": "Chard is a leafy green vegetable with a thick, sturdy stem and large, crinkled leaves. Common varieties include Swiss chard, French chard, and rainbow chard. It is a good source of vitamins A, C, and K, as well as iron and magnesium. Chard can be cooked or eaten raw, often used in salads, soups, and stir-fries. It thrives in cool temperatures and can be grown in both cool spring and fall seasons.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Answer: Chard is a leafy green vegetable with a thick, sturdy stem and large, crinkled leaves. Common varieties include Swiss chard, French chard, and rainbow chard. It is a good source of vitamins A, C, and K, as well as iron and magnesium. Chard can be cooked or eaten raw, often used in salads, soups, and stir-fries. It thrives in cool temperatures and can be grown in both cool spring and fall seasons.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nIdentify the type of vegetable: Chard is a leafy green vegetable.\nExplain its appearance: Chard has a thick, sturdy stem and large, crinkled leaves.\nList some common varieties: Swiss chard, French chard, and rainbow chard are popular varieties.\nDiscuss its nutritional benefits: It is a good source of vitamins A, C, and K, as well as iron and magnesium.\nMention its uses: Chard can be cooked or eaten raw, often used in salads, soups, and stir-fries.\nExplain its growing conditions: Chard thrives in cool temperatures and can be grown in both cool spring and fall seasons.\nExplain its growing conditions: Chard thrives in cool temperatures and can be grown in both cool spring and fall seasons.\n\nAnswer: Chard is a leafy green vegetable with a thick, sturdy stem and large, crinkled leaves. Common varieties include Swiss chard, French chard, and rainbow chard. It is a good source of vitamins A, C, and K, as well as iron and magnesium. Chard can be cooked or eaten raw, often used in salads, soups, and stir-fries. It thrives in cool temperatures and can be grown in both cool spring and fall seasons.", "time_sec": 61.532, "generation_time_seconds": 38.2761, "selection_time_seconds": 23.256, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [40, 1969, 6923, 264, 30078, 911, 1128, 279, 3405, 374, 10161, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-4.0718, -6.3256, -4.8656, -1.0612, -2.9682, -0.6491, -1.4202, -0.8278, -0.4461, -0.1586, -0.0069, -0.3896, -0.0691], "cumulative_logprob": -23.2598, "norm_logp": -1.7892, "input_len": 137, "output_tokens": 13}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 943, 315, 35481, 25, 910, 567, 374, 264, 15933, 88, 6176, 35481, 624, 840, 20772, 1181, 11094, 25, 910, 567, 702, 264, 12045, 11, 44552, 19101, 323, 3460, 11, 1560, 766, 832, 10901, 624, 852, 1045, 4185, 35580, 25, 29691, 521, 567, 11, 8585, 521, 567, 11, 323, 47613, 521, 567, 525, 5411, 35580, 624, 96554, 1181, 42126, 7567, 25, 1084, 374, 264, 1661, 2530, 315, 45092, 362, 11, 356, 11, 323, 730, 11, 438, 1632, 438, 11001, 323, 60833, 624, 44, 2939, 1181, 5711, 25, 910, 567, 646, 387, 29105, 476, 34561, 7112, 11, 3545, 1483, 304, 76752, 11, 5822, 1690, 11, 323, 23065, 2220, 4019, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.4825, -0.0039, -0.1724, -2.0434, -0.0017, -0.1386, -1.4664, -0.8029, -0.0