{"id": "460", "question": "how the colonists respond to the stamp act. why was it so upsetting?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the colonists responded to the Stamp Act of 1765 with a mix of violent and non-violent protests. They held peaceful protests, chanting slogans such as \"No Taxation without Representation,\" but these were often met with violence from British soldiers, known as Red Coats, who would come to cause fights and make arrests. The colonists also organized public protests, legislative resolutions, town meetings, and boycotts of British goods throughout the colonies. The Stamp Act was seen as particularly egregious because it was the first direct tax imposed by Parliament on the colonies, and it required the use of tax-stamped paper for all newspapers, legal documents, and other printed materials. The colonists felt that this was an overreach of British authority and a violation of their rights as British subjects. The Act was eventually repealed in 1766, but it had already sparked widespread outrage and resistance among the colonists, contributing to the growing tensions that would eventually lead to the American Revolution.", "pred": "The colonists responded to the Stamp Act by organizing protests, refusing to purchase taxed items, forming committees to discuss and decide on their next steps, petitioning the British Parliament to repeal the Act, engaging in acts of civil disobedience such as hiding and burning stamp paper, and boycotting British goods. The Stamp Act was so upsetting to the colonists because it imposed a new tax on all printed materials, including newspapers, legal documents, and playing cards, which was seen as an infringement on their rights as British citizens, as they believed they should not be taxed by a government they had no say in.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Answer: The colonists responded to the Stamp Act by organizing protests, refusing to purchase taxed items, forming committees to discuss and decide on their next steps, petitioning the British Parliament to repeal the Act, engaging in acts of civil disobedience such as hiding and burning stamp paper, and boycotting British goods. The Stamp Act was so upsetting to the colonists because it imposed a new tax on all printed materials, including newspapers, legal documents, and playing cards, which was seen as an infringement on their rights as British citizens, as they believed they should not be taxed by a government they had no say in.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nThe stamp act was so upsetting to the colonists because it imposed a new tax on all printed materials, including newspapers, legal documents, and playing cards. This was seen as an infringement on their rights as British citizens, as they believed they should not be taxed by a government they had no say in.\nNext logical reasoning step: The colonists respond to the Stamp Act by organizing protests and refusing to purchase taxed items. They also form committees to discuss and decide on their next steps. Additionally, they petition the British Parliament to repeal the Act.\nThe colonists' response to the Stamp Act was not limited to peaceful protests and petitions. They also engaged in acts of civil disobedience, such as hiding and burning stamp paper, and boycotting British goods to show their defiance of the tax. The Stamp Act also led to the formation of the Sons of Liberty, a group of revolutionary leaders who would play a crucial role in the American Revolution.\n\nAnswer: The colonists responded to the Stamp Act by organizing protests, refusing to purchase taxed items, forming committees to discuss and decide on their next steps, petitioning the British Parliament to repeal the Act, engaging in acts of civil disobedience such as hiding and burning stamp paper, and boycotting British goods. The Stamp Act was so upsetting to the colonists because it imposed a new tax on all printed materials, including newspapers, legal documents, and playing cards, which was seen as an infringement on their rights as British citizens, as they believed they should not be taxed by a government they had no say in.", "time_sec": 65.121, "generation_time_seconds": 37.8869, "selection_time_seconds": 27.2337, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [785, 21206, 1160, 572, 773, 89969, 311, 279, 14889, 1671, 1576, 432, 25980, 264, 501, 3742, 389, 678, 16709, 7236, 11, 2670, 31494, 11, 5777, 9293, 11, 323, 5619, 7411, 13, 1096, 572, 3884, 438, 458, 53343, 389, 862, 3188, 438, 7855, 10283, 11, 438, 807, 11585, 807, 1265, 537, 387, 71415, 553, 264, 3033, 807, 1030, 902, 1977, 304, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.2748, -4.6841, -0.0004, -0.3631, -0.3668, -0.0034, -0.8971, -0.017, -0.007, -0.0001, -0.0157, -0.0283, -1.3801, -0.4269, -3.8298, -0.0123, -0.1058, -2.3773, -0.0183, -0.0096, -0.2111, -0.2535, -0.016, -0.0278, -0.047, -0.0012, -0.0002, -0.0063, -0.7164, -0.0, -0.0798, -0.2388, -0.5786, -0.6565, -0.0007, -0.8919, -0.0999, -0.0023, -0.2176, -0.1014, -0.3346, -0.108, -0.2149, -0.7036, -0.2989, -0.2317, -0.1587, -0.834, -0.0614, -0.3308, -0.0798, -0.004, -0.7891, -0.291, -0.2143, -0.3988, -0.189, -0.0011, -2.2281, -0.0, -0.2024, -0.9713], "cumulative_logprob": -28.6104, "norm_logp": -0.4615, "input_len": 147, "output_tokens": 62}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 3151, 3565, 476, 10295, 504, 279, 46769, 429, 10339, 3170, 279, 54787, 3218, 572, 773, 89969, 311, 279, 14889, 1671, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.632, -0.0033, -0.1167, -0.4041, -0.8238, -1.627, -0.1794, -1.5236, -0.0, -0.8271, -0.1017, -0.3735, -1.1095, -0.001, -0.1853, -0.0, -0.0017, -0.0818, -0.0009, -0.0222, -0.0007, -0.0027, -0.0, -0.0332, -0.1214], "cumulative_logprob": -9.1726, "norm_logp": -0.3669, "input_len": 147, "output_tokens": 25}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [8304, 220, 16, 25, 64547, 279