{"id": "643", "question": "differences and similarities between red and white blood cells", "gt": "Based on the given passages, we can see that the primary difference between red and white blood cells is their respective functions within the body. Red blood cells are responsible for oxygen delivery to tissues, while white blood cells are part of the immune system and help combat diseases and viruses. However, both types of cells share some common features, such as being formed elements of blood, being formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow, having a cell membrane and cytoplasm, and circulating in the fluids in blood vessels, which is pumped by the heart.", "pred": "Differences and similarities between red and white blood cells include their structure, function, lifespan, and count.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Answer: Differences and similarities between red and white blood cells include their structure, function, lifespan, and count.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nExamine the structure of red and white blood cells.\nCompare their functions and roles in the body.\nExamine the physical characteristics and morphological differences between red and white blood cells.\n\nRed blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are both types of blood cells, but they have distinct differences and similarities.\n\nSimilarities:\n1. Both RBCs and WBCs are produced in the bone marrow.\n2. Both are involved in the circulatory system.\n3. Both are essential for maintaining the health of the body.\n\nDifferences:\n1. Structure: RBCs are biconcave discs, while WBCs are irregularly shaped and have a nucleus.\n2. Function: RBCs transport oxygen and carbon dioxide throughout the body, while WBCs fight infections and diseases.\n3. Lifespan: RBCs have a lifespan of about 120 days, while WBCs have a lifespan of about 1-3 days.\n4. Count: The average adult has about 5 million RBCs per microliter of blood, while the average adult has about 4,000 WBCs per microliter of blood.\n\nAnswer: Differences and similarities between red and white blood cells include their structure, function, lifespan, and count.", "time_sec": 46.259, "generation_time_seconds": 28.8715, "selection_time_seconds": 17.3875, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 1376, 6961, 17452, 315, 2518, 323, 4158, 6543, 7761, 11, 1741, 438, 862, 1379, 11, 6083, 11, 323, 9362, 315, 264, 61507, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.4351, -0.0056, -0.0833, -0.5921, -1.3032, -0.1044, -0.123, -0.1622, -0.7865, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -1.5891, -0.0782, -0.0, -0.8614, -0.8461, -0.0047, -0.0021, -0.0001, -0.0025, -0.804, -0.1119, -0.1156, -0.0011, -0.0228, -0.0217], "cumulative_logprob": -9.0567, "norm_logp": -0.3354, "input_len": 141, "output_tokens": 27}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [27374, 279, 6961, 17452, 315, 2518, 6543, 7761, 323, 4158, 6543, 7761, 151645], "logp_list": [-2.7743, -0.0495, -0.8113, -0.2346, -0.161, -0.0092, -1.2363, -0.0, -0.2455, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -4.1407], "cumulative_logprob": -9.6624, "norm_logp": -0.7433, "input_len": 141, "output_tokens": 13}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [19432, 279, 21085, 369, 3565, 389, 279, 6961, 17452, 315, 2518, 323, 4158, 6543, 7761, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-3.3993, -0.0174, -2.1329, -0.4711, -2.4357, -1.6986, -0.0227, -0.3467, -0.1036, -0.1554, -0.1112, -0.111, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.4748, -0.1494], "cumulative_logprob": -11.6298, "norm_logp": -0.6841, "input_len": 141, "output_tokens": 17}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [840, 19991, 279, 5944, 315, 2518, 323, 4158, 6543, 7761, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.7029, -0.0196, -0.0066, -0.9815, -1.1512, -0.0946, -0.6081, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.6095, -0.0394], "cumulative_logprob": -5.2134, "norm_logp": -0.4344, "input_len": 141, "output_tokens": 12}, {"phase": "vote", "beam": null, "token_ids": [785