{"id": "672", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it can be understood that the Weighted Average Costing (WAC) method is used to determine the average cost of inventory when a unit is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and uses it to calculate the weighted average cost per unit. WAC is commonly used when inventory items are intermingled and it's difficult to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nThe given question is asking about \"inventory costing weighted average.\" To answer this, we need to understand the concept of weighted average costing, which is a method used in inventory valuation. The reasoning steps would typically involve explaining what weighted average costing is, how it differs from other costing methods, and why it is used in inventory management.\nThe next logical step in the reasoning process would be to define the specific characteristics of the weighted average costing method used in inventory valuation. This involves explaining how weighted average costing works by averaging the cost of all items in inventory, regardless of the order in which they were received. It helps to smooth out price fluctuations and provide a more stable value for inventory purposes.\nThe next logical reasoning step would be to apply the weighted average costing method to a practical scenario. This involves creating an example or a hypothetical situation to demonstrate how the method is used in inventory management. By providing a specific example, the reasoning would be more concrete and easier to understand.\n\nUnable to answe