{"id": "673", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "Sure! Here's my answer to the question:\n\nThe weighted average method of costing inventory assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. When a unit of inventory is sold, the weighted average method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns that average price to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change.\n\nTo calculate the weighted average cost per unit, divide the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale. The weighted-average figure can then be used to assign costs to both ending inventory and cost of goods sold. The recorded amount of inventory on hand using the weighted average costing method represents a value between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\n\nWeighted average costing is commonly used in situations where inventory items are intermingled and it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "pred": "Weighted average inventory costing is an inventory valuation method where the cost of ending inventory and the cost of goods sold are determined by taking the average cost of all inventory items available for sale during the period. This method involves calculating the average cost per unit by summing the total cost of all units available for sale and dividing by the total number of units. The average cost per unit is then used to determine the cost of ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. For example, if a company has 100 units of inventory at a cost of $10 each and purchases 200 more units at a cost of $12 each, the average cost per unit would be ($10 x 100 + $12 x 200) / (100 + 200) = $11.60. This average cost of $11.60 would then be used to value the ending inventory and the cost of goods sold.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Answer: Weighted average inventory costing is an inventory valuation method where the cost of ending inventory and the cost of goods sold are determined by taking the average cost of all inventory items available for sale during the period. This method involves calculating the average cost per unit by summing the total cost of all units available for sale and dividing by the total number of units. The average cost per unit is then used to determine the cost of ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. For example, if a company has 100 units of inventory at a cost of $10 each and purchases 200 more units at a cost of $12 each, the average cost per unit would be ($10 x 100 + $12 x 200) / (100 + 200) = $11.60. This average cost of $11.60 would then be used to value the ending inventory and the cost of goods sold.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nStep 1: Understand the concept of weighted average inventory costing.\n- Weighted average inventory costing is an inventory valuation method where the cost of ending inventory and the cost of goods sold are determined by taking the average cost of all inventory items available for sale during the period.\nStep 2: Identify key components of the weighted average inventory costing method.\n- Determine how the average cost is calculated.\n- Understand the process of applying this method to track inventory costs.\nStep 3: Explore the application of the weighted average inventory costing method in a practical scenario.\n- Show how the method is used to calculate the average cost per unit.\n- Demonstrate how this average cost is then applied to the units on hand and units sold throughout the period.\n- Provide an example using specific numbers to illustrate the calculation process.\n\nAnswer: Weighted average inventory costing is an inventory valuation method where the cost of ending inventory and the cost of goods sold are determined by taking the average cost of all inventory items available for sale during the period. This method involves calculating the average cost per unit by summing the total cost of all units available for sale and dividing by the total number of units. The average cost per unit is then used to determine the cost of ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. For example, if a company has 100 units of inventory at a cost of $10 each and purchases 200 more units at a cost of $12 each, the average cost per unit would be ($10 x 100 + $12 x 200) / (100 + 200) = $11.60. This average cost of $11.60 would then be used to value the ending inventory and the cost of goods sold.", "time_sec": 53.807, "generation_time_seconds": 38.35, "selection_time_seconds": 15.4565, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [5501, 3410, 279, 21085, 476, 3151, 3565, 911, 364, 31093, 53724, 36824, 5461, 6, 429, 582, 646, 23643, 311, 4226, 279, 3405, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-3.4304, -0.0397, -0.0044, -1.6384, -0.5316, -5.4819, -0.3383, -0.546, -7.3392, -0.0062, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0076, -2.2763, -4.4576, -0.1395, -0.5711, -1.5733, -1.2139, -0.0083, -0.0023, -0.0122, -0.0347], "cumulative_logprob": -29.653, "norm_logp": -1.2355, "input_len": 136, "output_tokens": 24}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [8304, 220, 16, 25, 70894, 279, 7286, 315, 36824, 5461, 15444, 53724, 624, 12, 16516, 291, 5461, 15444, 53724, 374, 458, 15444, 50162, 1714, 1380, 279, 2783, 315, 13391, 15444, 323, 279, 2783, 315, 11561, 6088, 525, 10838, 553, 4633, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 15444, 3589, 2500, 369, 6278, 2337, 279, 4168, 382], "logp_list": [-2.5376, -0.0089, -0.0978, -0.0012, -1.3328, -0.0176, -1.2146, -0.0039, -0.9064, -0.0001, -0.0732, -0.0014, -0.4694, -2.4849, -0.1596, 0.0, -0.0003, -0.0899, -0.0007, -0.0156, -0.4608, -0.4609, -0.2167, -0.0003, -0.3971, -0.0054, -0.0751, -0.0031, -1.0438, -0.0, -0.3433, -0.4623, -0.0001, 0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0293,