{"id": "240", "question": "how do crab traps work", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a crab trap is a large square metal cage with a system of nets inside that allows crabs to enter through a narrow slit at the top. To set the trap, the ends with lead-ins are placed in the water and the crabs are attracted to the free meal offered by the trap. The Alaska Department of Fish and Game recommends using king crab pots that range from 6 by 6 feet to 8 by 8 feet with heights between 2 and 3 feet. Over time, these mechanisms may disable the trap, preventing it from ghost fishing.", "pred": "Crab traps are designed to capture crabs by using a combination of entrance and interior mechanisms. The entrance of the crab trap is typically a small opening that allows crabs to enter but is too narrow for them to exit. This entrance is often baited with food to attract the crabs. Once a crab enters the trap, it is held in place by a series of bars or grids that prevent it from escaping. The interior of the trap is designed to keep the crab secure until it is ready to be harvested.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Crab traps are designed to capture crabs by using a combination of entrance and interior mechanisms. The entrance of the crab trap is typically a small opening that allows crabs to enter but is too narrow for them to exit. This entrance is often baited with food to attract the crabs. Once a crab enters the trap, it is held in place by a series of bars or grids that prevent it from escaping. The interior of the trap is designed to keep the crab secure until it is ready to be harvested.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nExamine the structure and design of the crab trap to understand how it captures crabs.\nAnalyze the entrance of the crab trap to understand the mechanism that draws crabs in.\nExamine the interior of the crab trap to understand the mechanisms that hold and secure the crabs once captured.\n\nCrab traps are designed to capture crabs by using a combination of entrance and interior mechanisms. The entrance of the crab trap is typically a small opening that allows crabs to enter but is too narrow for them to exit. This entrance is often baited with food to attract the crabs. Once a crab enters the trap, it is held in place by a series of bars or grids that prevent it from escaping. The interior of the trap is designed to keep the crab secure until it is ready to be harvested.", "time_sec": 60.981, "generation_time_seconds": 32.0167, "selection_time_seconds": 28.9643, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [2082, 55856, 279, 6961, 6813, 315, 264, 59412, 22862, 11, 1741, 438, 279, 5944, 11, 48549, 11, 323, 48060, 21448, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-2.2943, -0.0, -0.0265, -0.7799, -0.6714, -0.824, -0.2061, -0.0679, -0.0, -1.4284, -0.1196, 0.0, -0.0737, -4.643, -0.3397, -1.9259, -0.0003, -0.0016, -1.5685, -0.957, -0.1265, -0.0076], "cumulative_logprob": -16.0619, "norm_logp": -0.7301, "input_len": 137, "output_tokens": 22}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [840, 19991, 279, 5944, 323, 6813, 315, 264, 59412, 22862, 438, 7481, 304, 279, 21085, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.8657, -0.1111, -0.0248, -1.1896, -0.0823, -0.4208, -0.0055, -0.2865, -0.7948, -0.0, -0.8627, -0.0196, -0.0068, -0.0001, -0.5806, -0.0722, -0.1049], "cumulative_logprob": -5.428, "norm_logp": -0.3193, "input_len": 137, "output_tokens": 17}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [840, 19991, 279, 5944, 323, 2884, 315, 279, 59412, 22862, 311, 3535, 1246, 432, 40155, 1560, 3435, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.8657, -0.1111, -0.0248, -1.1896, -0.0823, -1.6708, -0.0153, -0.8505, -0.0028, -0.252, -1.5105, -0.08, -0.4051, -0.0103, -0.1708, -0.0327, 0.0, -0.0375, -0.0117], "cumulative_logprob": -7.3235, "norm_logp": -0.3854, "input_len": 137, "output_tokens": 19}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 