{"id": "302", "question": "how to make hot water heater circulate", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to make a hot water heater circulate:\n\nOption 1: Install a recirculating pump. Passage 3 explains that recirculating pumps can rapidly pull hot water from the water heater and send cool water from the hot water lines back to the water heater to be reheated and reused. These pumps can be controlled by a button, timer, or motion sensor.\n\nOption 2: Use a hot water circulation pump. Passage 2 describes how a hot water circulation pump can send cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line, ensuring that hot water is always available when needed.\n\nIt is important to note that running a hot water circulation pump continuously can consume a significant amount of energy, as mentioned in passage 1. Therefore, it may be more cost-effective to only run the pump when needed, such as when you turn on the faucet.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nThe physical details of a hot water heater's circulation system are not provided in the given passages.\nThe passages do not contain sufficient information about the physical details of a hot water heater's circulation system.\nThe physical details of a hot water heater's circulation system are not provided in the given passages.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 46.651, "generation_time_seconds": 27.6795, "selection_time_seconds": 18.9711, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [840, 20772, 279, 6770, 16953, 315, 264, 4017, 3015, 42238, 323, 1246, 34755, 13666, 17712, 476, 1526, 22148, 3363, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.7367, -2.0957, -0.4451, -1.1267, -3.1536, -0.0156, -0.6981, -0.1861, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.538