{"id": "409", "question": "how to make panko crumbs", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to make panko crumbs:\n\nPassage 1 states to push torn pieces of bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Then, spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake in a preheated oven until they are dry but not toasted, about 6 to 8 minutes.\n\nPassage 2 provides similar instructions, stating to push chunks of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Then, spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake at 300°F until the crumbs are dry but not toasted, about 6 to 8 minutes.\n\nPassage 3 provides additional information, suggesting to preheat the oven to 300°F before making the crumbs. The passage also recommends using a blender carafe to pulse the bread until coarse crumbs form, and then spreading the crumbs evenly on two half-sheet pans and baking until they just begin to brown and are crisp, about 5 minutes. After cooling the crumbs on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes, transfer them to an airtight container and store them at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\n\nTherefore, to make panko crumbs based on the provided passages, you can use a food processor or blender to shred or pulse bread into coarse crumbs, and then bake them in a preheated oven until they are dry but not toasted.", "pred": "To make panko crumbs, you need to break up bread into small pieces using a sharp knife or a food processor. This step is crucial because it ensures that the panko crumbs have a consistent texture and size, which is essential for achieving the desired crispy texture when used in recipes.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "To make panko crumbs, you need to break up bread into small pieces using a sharp knife or a food processor. This step is crucial because it ensures that the panko crumbs have a consistent texture and size, which is essential for achieving the desired crispy texture when used in recipes.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nTo make panko crumbs, you need to follow these steps:\nBreak up bread into small pieces using a sharp knife or a food processor.\nExplain why breaking up the bread is important for the texture and size of the panko.\n\nTo make panko crumbs, you need to break up bread into small pieces using a sharp knife or a food processor. This step is crucial because it ensures that the panko crumbs have a consistent texture and size, which is essential for achieving the desired crispy texture when used in recipes.", "time_sec": 45.274, "generation_time_seconds": 28.5754, "selection_time_seconds": 16.698, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [10850, 1526, 279, 2661, 46769, 323, 8649, 279, 9760, 1995, 911, 3259, 281, 92852, 96577, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-7.4571, -0.2163, -0.0304, -0.7798, -0.114, -0.5841, -3.8327, -1.1017, -0.3361, -0.0449, -0.2858, -0.0818, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.004, -0.0184, -0.3304], "cumulative_logprob": -15.2181, "norm_logp": -0.8952, "input_len": 138, "output_tokens": 17}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [1249, 1281, 281, 92852, 96577, 11, 498, 646, 1191, 553, 96039, 287, 279, 16002, 304, 9255, 3015, 11, 1221, 55736, 476, 14376, 432, 1119, 2613, 9666, 13, 9295, 11, 23604, 279, 16002, 9666, 1119, 264, 25600, 12000, 315, 14074, 476, 714, 4991, 33163, 311, 60692, 1105, 3080, 807, 525, 41047, 20507, 6758, 13, 4636, 77346, 11, 26025, 1007, 894, 13623, 14473, 323, 1992, 279, 16002, 9666, 1119, 264, 19212, 315, 281, 92852, 85566, 13, 19219, 279, 16002, 9666, 448, 279, 281, 92852, 3080, 807, 525, 1632, 48382, 13, 1446, 646, 1083, 912, 264, 2699, 315, 5590, 476, 49359, 14100, 311, 279, 281, 92852, 311, 1492, 279, 16002, 9666, 9214, 3786, 13, 17375, 11, 8317, 279, 48382, 16002, 9666, 311, 264, 27815, 10834, 323, 22544, 1105, 304, 279, 23387], "logp_list": [-2.8589, -0.3818, -0.002, -0.0, -0.0286, -