{"id": "443", "question": "how are forest fires beneficial to conifers like jack pines?", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, forest fires are beneficial to conifers like jack pines in several ways:\n\n1. Fire helps to clear the way for new growth by consuming dead, decaying vegetation on the forest floor, creating a clean slate for seed germination and seedling establishment (Passage 2).\n2. Jack pine seeds are adapted to fire, as they are stored in serotinous cones that only open after a fire has melted the resin holding them closed. This allows the seeds to disperse and establish new populations (Passage 2).\n3. Fire can create a favorable environment for jack pine and aspen regrowth by reducing competition from other tree species, such as spruce, which have better access to sunlight while shading the other trees (Passage 3).\n\nTherefore, forest fires can benefit jack pines by providing a means of seed dispersal and creating conditions that promote their growth and regeneration.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nExamine the specific examples or numbers provided in the passages that explain the beneficial effects of forest fires on conifers. Look for information about how fires can aid in the growth, reproduction, and survival of conifer species.\nLook for information about the specific processes or mechanisms through which fires benefit jack pines and other conifers. Consider factors like seed dispersal, removal of deadwood, and promotion of new growth.\nIdentify any comparisons or contrasts between forest fires and other environmental factors that may affect conifers similarly. This will help in understanding the unique advantages that fires provide.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 60.476, "generation_time_seconds": 29.7915, "selection_time_seconds": 30.6845, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 49853, 3476, 315, 13638, 26084, 304, 390, 11437, 782, 60851, 11, 21080, 389, 1246, 807, 27596, 10320, 78735, 278, 323, 2138, 369, 59417, 315, 390, 11437, 9419, 1075, 25072, 281, 1543, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-2.1926, -0.0039, -0.2934, -5.4131, -0.4251, -0.0724, -0.4985, -0.0003, -0.0671, -0.9072, -0.0004, -0.5483, -0.6152, -0.7642, -0.6827, -0.0487, -0.9015, -0.0708, -3.7858, -2.0292, -0.1502, -0.0, -0.2502, -7.1056, -0.2011, -3.2649, -0.3024, -0.5776, -0.2946, -0.6195, -1.2222, -0.0488, -0.0233, 0.0, -0.0071, -0.0038], "cumulative_logprob": -33.3917, "norm_logp": -0.9275, "input_len": 144, "output_tokens": 36}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [840, 19991, 279, 3151, 10295, 476, 5109, 3897, 304, 279, 46769, 429, 10339, 279, 23699, 6239, 315, 13638, 26084, 389, 390, 98813, 13, 9192, 369, 1995, 911, 1246, 26084, 646, 12296, 304, 279, 6513, 11, 38556, 11, 323, 19661, 315, 390, 11437, 9419, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.1211, -0.0332, -0.0603, -0.462, -0.5189, -0.5078, -1.1999, -0.4388, -0.0357, -0.0001, -1.134, -0.8563, -1.3535, -1.1185, -1.2496, -0.9081, -0.0032, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.4312, -0.0096, -2.496, -2.7837, -0.0002, -3.5916, -1.2342, -0.2097, -0.3337, -0.4896, -3.2009, -0.064, -0.3164, -1.2332, -1.7382, -0.2755, -0.0001, -0.9966, -0.3238, -0.0001, -0.6421, -1.7899, -0.0717, -1.3733, -0.1297], "cumulative_logprob": -34.7366, "norm_logp": -0.7719, "input_len": 144, "output_tokens": 45}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [840, 19991, 279, 3151, 10295, 11, 5109, 11, 476, 35495, 2661, 304, 279, 21085, 8826, 279, 6239, 315, 13638, 26084, 389, 390, 98813, 1075, 25072, 281, 1543, 13, 64547, 894, 3565, 429, 7512, 279, 7567, 315, 13638, 26084, 369, 1493, 12408, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.1211, -0.0332, -0.0603, -0.462, -0.5189, -1.0435, -0.0001, -0.0023, -0.0014, -0.0001, -2.7984, -0.0408, -0.