{"id": "732", "question": "how do you process the air you breathe before it gets to your lungs?", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the process of processing the air we breathe before it reaches the lungs involves the following steps:\n\n1. The diaphragm muscle contracts to take air into the lungs, moving down from the bottom of the rib cage towards the abdominal region. (Passage 1)\n2. The process of breathing involves the exchange of gases between the body and the outside environment, occurring in the lungs. (Passage 2)\n3. Increasing the volume of the thoracic cavity, including the lungs, decreases the air pressure in the lungs below the atmospheric pressure, forcing air from the atmosphere into the lungs, causing us to inhale. (Passage 3)\n\nTherefore, the main steps in processing the air we breathe before it reaches the lungs are:\n\n1. Contraction of the diaphragm muscle to draw air into the lungs.\n2. Exchange of gases between the body and the outside environment in the lungs.\n3. Expansion of the thoracic cavity to decrease air pressure in the lungs and facilitate inhalation.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "The process of air processing before it reaches the lungs involves several organs and steps. The air you breathe in first passes through the nose, where it is warmed, moistened, and filtered. Next, the air travels down the trachea, which is a tube that connects the nose and mouth to the lungs. The trachea is lined with cilia, which are tiny hair-like structures that help to move mucus and other particles out of the airway. The air then passes through the bronchi, which are smaller tubes that branch off from the trachea and lead to the lungs. The bronchi are also lined with cilia, which help to move mucus and other particles out of the airway. Finally, the air reaches the alveoli, which are tiny air sacs in the lungs where gas exchange occurs. The alveoli are surrounded by capillaries, which are tiny blood vessels that allow oxygen to pass from the air into the bloodstream and carbon dioxide to pass from the bloodstream into the air.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "The process of air processing before it reaches the lungs involves several organs and steps. The air you breathe in first passes through the nose, where it is warmed, moistened, and filtered. Next, the air travels down the trachea, which is a tube that connects the nose and mouth to the lungs. The trachea is lined with cilia, which are tiny hair-like structures that help to move mucus and other particles out of the airway. The air then passes through the bronchi, which are smaller tubes that branch off from the trachea and lead to the lungs. The bronchi are also lined with cilia, which help to move mucus and other particles out of the airway. Finally, the air reaches the alveoli, which are tiny air sacs in the lungs where gas exchange occurs. The alveoli are surrounded by capillaries, which are tiny blood vessels that allow oxygen to pass from the air into the bloodstream and carbon dioxide to pass from the bloodstream into the air.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nIdentify the organs involved in processing the air before it reaches the lungs.\nIdentify the sequence of organs and processes involved in processing the air before it reaches the lungs, starting from the nose and moving to the lungs.\nAnalyze the function and role of each organ in the process of air processing before it reaches the lungs.\n\nThe process of air processing before it reaches the lungs involves several organs and steps. The air you breathe in first passes through the nose, where it is warmed, moistened, and filtered. Next, the air travels down the trachea, which is a tube that connects the nose and mouth to the lungs. The trachea is lined with cilia, which are tiny hair-like structures that help to move mucus and other particles out of the airway. The air then passes through the bronchi, which are smaller tubes that branch off from the trachea and lead to the lungs. The bronchi are also lined with cilia, which help to move mucus and other particles out of the airway. Finally, the air reaches the alveoli, which are tiny air sacs in the lungs where gas exchange occurs. The alveoli are surrounded by capillaries, which are tiny blood vessels that allow oxygen to pass from the air into the bloodstream and carbon dioxide to pass from the bloodstream into the air.", "time_sec": 57.774, "generation_time_seconds": 29.9863, "selection_time_seconds": 27.7875, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [785, 3405, 17064, 911, 279, 1882, 279, 3720, 5780, 1526, 1573, 432, 24491, 279, 44174, 13, 16246, 419, 11, 279, 1790, 19819, 3019, 1035, 387, 311, 10542, 279, 35753, 323, 14389, 6398, 304, 419, 1882, 13, 4220, 2924, 279, 19142, 11, 10780, 11, 27591, 11, 489, 1777, 64, 11, 323, 29971, 14604, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.0489, -0.872, -0.0526, -0.0969, -0.0412, -0.0173, -5.1269, -0.0029, -3.2556, -0.0, -0.0017, -0.4012, -0.0014, -0.056, -0.0, -0.0165, -4.7215, -2.1293, -0.0341, -0.2092, -0.4146, -0.0332, -0.0939, -1.1414, -0.0057, -0.0021, -0.3754, -0.1942, -0.5045, -0.4607, -0.4523, -0.1433, -0.0001, -0.2232, -0.0086, -0.6198, -2.734, -0.9478, -0.0058, -0.1144, -0.0045, -2.0781, -0.0023, -1.3453, -0.0277, -0.4355, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0011, -0.7866, -0.0244, -0.02, -0.4178, -0.2026], "cumulative_logprob": -31.9061, "norm_logp": -0.5909, "input_len": 146, "output_tokens": 54}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 729, 315, 279, 71088, 55329, 304, 279, 3720, 8692, 1573, 432, 24491, 279, 44174, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.9417, -0.0047, -0.0102, -7.3481, -0.0652, -0.0501, -1.1697, -0.1967, -0.7453, -0.7376, -2.6781, -0.0769, -1.3176, -0.1348, -0.021, -0.0014, -0.0, -0.0205, -0.001], "cumulative_logprob": -16.5206, "norm_logp": -0.8695, "input_len": 146, "output_tokens": 19}, {"p