{"id": "126", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "gt": "Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site, cutting and assembling the skids, flooring, and walls. To prepare the site, a 4\" layer of compacted gravel is recommended. The skids are cut to 70 2/4\" and set and leveled according to the shed building plans. Two 2 x 6 rim joists are cut at 7 3/4\" and six joists at 44 3/4\". The floor is assembled and set on the skids, checked for square, and anchored to the skids with four joist clip angles. The walls are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame. Pressure-treated 4 by 4s are used for the skids, while 2 by 4s are used for the rim joists and flooring. 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood is attached to the frame for the flooring.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Answer:[Unable to answer based on given passages]", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nAnswer:[Unable to answer based on given passages]\nStep 1: Understanding the context\n- The question is about building a lean-to shed.\n- A lean-to is a simple structure that is attached to a wall, typically using the wall as one of its supporting sides.\nStep 2: Identifying key elements of a lean-to shed\n- A lean-to shed typically consists of a roof and walls.\n- The walls are usually made of siding, which can be wooden or other materials.\n- The roof is usually pitched and covered with shingles, tiles, or other roofing materials.\n- The lean-to is often constructed using basic construction techniques such as framing, nailing, and siding.\n\nAnswer:[Unable to answer based on given passages]", "time_sec": 50.167, "generation_time_seconds": 36.6495, "selection_time_seconds": 13.5178, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 7236, 4362, 369, 4752, 264, 15651, 4686, 24354, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.37