{"id": "203", "question": "what causes necrotizing fasciitis", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the main cause of necrotizing fasciitis is bacterial infection, specifically group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes) and Staphylococcus. Additionally, other types of bacteria have also been associated with the disease. Trauma, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle puncture wounds, can also play a role in the development of necrotizing fasciitis. Surgical incision sites and various surgical procedures can also serve as a source of infection. Therefore, the answer is: Bacterial infection, including group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes) and Staphylococcus, and trauma.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nThe given question asks about the causes of necrotizing fasciitis. The reasoning steps should focus on identifying the risk factors or conditions that lead to this condition. The next logical step would be to look for information in the passage that mentions the causes or risk factors associated with necrotizing fasciitis.\nThe next logical step is to examine the passage for any mention of the causes or risk factors associated with necrotizing fasciitis. This will help identify the specific factors that can lead to the development of this condition.\nThe next logical step is to analyze the passage for any specific details or examples that describe the causes or risk factors of necrotizing fasciitis. This will provide a clear understanding of what factors contribute to the development of this condition.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 62.346, "generation_time_seconds": 36.916, "selection_time_seconds": 25.43, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0,