{"id": "383", "question": "how to arrange an excel sheet alphabetical", "gt": "To arrange an excel sheet alphabetical, one can follow these steps:\n\n1. Select the Data tab from the toolbar at the top of the screen.\n2. Click on the Sort button and select Custom Sort from the popup menu.\n3. When the Sort window appears, select the data that you wish to sort by. In this example, we want to sort by the Product column (column B) in alphabetical order (A to Z).\n4. Click on the OK button.\n5. The data should now be sorted in alphabetical order.", "pred": "10. If additional columns or rows need to be sorted alphabetically, repeat the above steps for each set of data.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "10. If additional columns or rows need to be sorted alphabetically, repeat the above steps for each set of data.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nTo arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, follow these steps:\n1. Select the data you want to arrange. This can be an entire column, row, or range of cells containing the data you wish to organize alphabetically.\n2. Go to the \"Data\" tab on the Excel ribbon at the top of the screen. This tab is typically located under the \"Home\" tab but can be accessed by clicking on the \"Data\" tab directly.\n3. In the \"Data\" tab, look for the \"Sort\" button. It will be represented by a small icon that looks like an arrow pointing down. Click on it.\n4\n4. A drop-down menu will appear with several sorting options. Choose the option that sorts the selected data in alphabetical order. Typically, this will be labeled as \"A to Z\" or \"Ascending\".\n5. Click on the chosen sorting option to apply the alphabetical arrangement to your selected data. Excel will sort the data in ascending order, placing the alphabetically arranged data in the order from A to Z.\n6. Verify that the data has been sorted correctly. If you selected a range of cells, check if the entire range has been sorted alphabetically or only a part of it.\n7. If needed, repeat the steps for additional\nIf you want to sort in descending order, choose the option labeled \"Z to A\" or \"Descending\" in the sorting menu.\n8. Click on the chosen option to sort the data in descending alphabetical order. This will place the alphabetically arranged data in the order from Z to A.\n9. Verify that the data has been sorted correctly in the descending order.\n10. If additional columns or rows need to be sorted alphabetically, repeat the above steps for each set of data.\n\nAnswer: To arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, follow these steps:\n1. Select the data you want to arrange. This can be an entire column, row, or range of cells containing the data you wish to organize alphabetically.\n2. Go to the \"Data\" tab on the Excel ribbon at the top of the screen. This tab is typically located under the \"Home\" tab but can be accessed by clicking on the \"Data\" tab directly.\n3. In the \"Data\" tab, look for the \"Sort\" button. It will be represented by a small icon that looks like an arrow pointing down. Click on it.\n4. A drop-down menu will appear with several sorting options. Choose the option that sorts the selected data in alphabetical order. Typically, this will be labeled as \"A to Z\" or \"Ascending\".\n5. Click on the chosen sorting option to apply the alphabetical arrangement to your selected data. Excel will sort the data in ascending order, placing the alphabetically arranged data in the order from A to Z.\n6. Verify that the data has been sorted correctly. If you selected a range of cells, check if the entire range has been sorted alphabetically or only a part of it.\n7. If needed, repeat the steps for additional\nIf you want to sort in descending order, choose the option labeled \"Z to A\" or \"Descending\" in the sorting menu.\n8. Click on the chosen option to sort the data in descending alphabetical order. This will place the alphabetically arranged data in the order from Z to A.\n9. Verify that the data has been sorted correctly in the descending order.\n10. If additional columns or rows need to be sorted alphabetically, repeat the above steps for each set of data.", "time_sec": 79.942, "generation_time_seconds": 60.0628, "selection_time_seconds": 19.8788, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [785, 3405, 17064, 1246, 311, 30893, 458, 21029, 10834, 27790, 2673, 13, 2014, 4226, 419, 11, 582, 1184, 311, 1477, 1995, 304, 279, 2661, 46769, 911, 28273, 476, 76118, 821, 304, 21029, 13, 1096, 5646, 3330, 369, 11221, 389, 1246, 311, 990, 21029, 594, 28273, 5746, 323, 894, 3151, 7354, 476, 5413, 9733, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.9342, -0.6009, -0.0663, -0.6161, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.0114, -0.0001, -0.0033, -0.0, -0.1348, -0.4616, -0.7202, -0.0105, -0.0218, -0.3102, -0.0261, -0.0073, -4.948, -0.3308, -0.8715, -0.0, -0.2258, -0.0471, -0.4337, -0.4959, -1.2672, -0.081, -0.0153, -0.4013, -0.0297, -0.7414, -5.1316, -2.0471, -1.1732, -0.0004, -0.7493, -1.3713, -1.0891, -0.0001, -1.461, -0.2443, -0.0545, -0.3842, -0.7485, -2.7356, -0.2647, -0.3738, -0.4364, -0.0956, -3.9666, -0.3088, -2.8413, -0.3948], "cumulative_logprob": -40.6862, "norm_logp": -0.7397, "input_len": 139, "output_tokens": 55}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [1249, 30893, 458, 21029, 10834, 27790, 2673, 11, 498, 1184, 311, 3378, 279, 821, 304, 264, 3151, 3250, 13, 5692, 525, 279, 11682, 7354, 1447], "logp_list": [-0.952, -0.2092, -0.0009, -0.008, -0.0001, -0.0025, -0.0, -0.0153, -0.7442, -0.9688, -0.0006, -1.9329, -0.0026, -0.0073, -0.7023, -2.2746, -0.0686, -0.0463, -0.2928, -0.5411, -0.8574, -0.0008, -4.0348, -0.0033, -0.3797], "cumulative_logprob": -14.0461, "norm_logp": -0.5618, "input_len": 139, "output_tokens": 25}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [1249, 30893, 458, 21029, 10834, 27790, 2673, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 510, 16, 13, 8427, 279, 821, 498, 1366, 311, 30893, 13, 1096