{"id": "646", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "To plant a potato that has sprouted, follow these steps:\n\n1. Cut large, sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each using a sharp knife.\n2. Set the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet and allow them to dry out for a cool, dark location overnight.\n3. Once the cut ends are dry, thoroughly wash the potatoes.\n4. Plant the potatoes in the ground, either whole or cut into cubes with a sprout on each cube.\n5. Space the potatoes about 6 to 8 inches apart and cover them with a layer of compost.\n6. Replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\n7. Water the potatoes regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\n8. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.", "pred": "To plant a sprouted potato, first examine the sprouted potato for healthy buds and any signs of disease or damage. Choose a suitable planting location with well-draining soil and full sunlight. Prepare the soil by loosening it and removing any debris or weeds that could compete with your potato plant.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, first examine the sprouted potato for healthy buds and any signs of disease or damage. Choose a suitable planting location with well-draining soil and full sunlight. Prepare the soil by loosening it and removing any debris or weeds that could compete with your potato plant.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nExamine the sprouted potato for healthy buds and any signs of disease or damage.\nChoose a suitable planting location with well-draining soil and full sunlight.\nPrepare the soil by loosening it and removing any debris or weeds that could compete with your potato plant.\n\nAnswer: To plant a sprouted potato, first examine the sprouted potato for healthy buds and any signs of disease or damage. Ch