{"id": "283", "question": "difference between an adverb clause and an adjective clause", "gt": "Based on the given passages, an adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective or another adverb, while an adjective clause modifies a noun or pronoun.", "pred": "Adverb clauses and adjective clauses serve different functions in a sentence. Adverb clauses modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverb clauses, providing information about when, where, why, how, or to what extent an action occurs. For example, \"I will go to the store tomorrow\" is an adverb clause modifying the verb \"go.\" Adjective clauses, on the other hand, modify nouns or pronouns, providing information about the qualities or characteristics of the noun or pronoun. For example, \"The book that I read last night is very interesting\" is an adjective clause modifying the noun \"book.\" Adverb clauses are introduced by subordinating conjunctions such as \"when,\" \"where,\" \"why,\" \"how,\" \"because,\" \"although,\" \"since,\" \"until,\" \"while,\" \"as,\" \"though,\" \"in order that,\" \"so that,\" \"so,\" \"nor,\" \"neither,\" \"either,\" \"as well as,\" \"as well,\" \"as much as,\" \"as far as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,\" \"as long as,\" \"as soon as,