{"id": "296", "question": "what are examples of potential and kinetic energy?", "gt": "Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane in flight, due to its large mass and fast velocity, and a baseball thrown by a pitcher, which can have a large amount of kinetic energy due to its fast velocity. Examples of potential energy include gravitational potential energy, which is the energy stored in an object as the result of its vertical position or height, and elastic potential energy. Other activities like a car moving, a ball rolling, a river flowing, an athlete running, rides in an amusement park, and a satellite in orbit also involve potential and kinetic energy.", "pred": "Potential energy examples: stretched rubber band, book on a shelf. Kinetic energy examples: moving car, falling apple.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Answer: Potential energy examples: stretched rubber band, book on a shelf. Kinetic energy examples: moving car, falling apple.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nIdentify the types of energy mentioned in the question: potential energy and kinetic energy.\nLook for examples of potential energy and kinetic energy in the provided passages.\nAnalyze how potential energy and kinetic energy are defined and described in the passages to identify specific examples.\n\nPotential energy is the energy stored in an object due to its position or state. An example of potential energy is a stretched rubber band. When the rubber band is stretched, it has potential energy because it can do work when released. Another example is a book on a shelf. The book has potential energy because it can fall and do work when it is released.\n\nKinetic energy is the energy of motion. An example of kinetic energy is a moving car. The car has kinetic energy because it is in motion and can do work by moving objects or causing damage. Another example is a falling apple. The apple has kinetic energy because it is in motion and can do work by hitting the ground or other objects.\n\nAnswer: Potential energy examples: stretched rubber band, book on a shelf. Kinetic energy examples: moving car, falling apple.", "time_sec": 38.983, "generation_time_seconds": 27.2414, "selection_time_seconds": 11.7413, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 3151, 10295, 315, 4650, 4802, 323, 70323, 4802, 504, 279, 2661, 46769, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.0911, -0.008, -2.53, -0.0327, -0.2707, -0.0515, -0.252, -1.132, -0.0023, -0.0, -0.5112, -0.0294, -0.1627, -0.0107, -0.0584, -0.0568], "cumulative_logprob": -6.1995, "norm_logp": -0.3875, "input_len": 140, "output_tokens": 16}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 10295, 315, 4650, 4802, 504, 279, 2661, 46769, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.0911, -0.008, -1.1014, -0.0592, -0.0571, -0.0195, -0.6186, -0.0089, -0.7051, -0.1095, -0.0632, -0.0599], "cumulative_logprob": -3.9015, "norm_logp": -0.3251, "input_len": 140, "output_tokens": 12}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [28301, 1437, 279, 4494, 315, 4802, 9733, 304, 279, 3405, 25, 4650, 4802, 323, 70323, 4802, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.0911, -0.008, -0.9229, -0.3667, -0.0001, -0.0346, -0.0714, -0.0645, -0.0005, -0.0283, -0.4616, -0.0608, -0.3985, -0.0016, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0208, -0.0497], "cumulative_logprob": -3.5811, "norm_logp": -0.1989, "input_len": 140, "output_tokens": 18}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [5338, 11, 10542, 279, 4494, 315, 4802, 9733, 304, 279, 3405, 13, 576, 3405, 17064, 911, 4650, 323, 70323, 4802, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-3.859, -0.0229, -0.4908, -0.1397, -0.6012, -0.0001, -0.0039, -0.029, -0.0713, -0.0001, -0.0089, -1.8599, -0.4643, -0.0092, -0.1654, -1.5108, -0.0944, -0.0232, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0733, -0.4619], "cumulative_logprob": -9.8893, "norm_logp": -0.4495, "input_len": 140, "output_tokens": 22}, {"phase": "vote", "beam": null, "token_ids": [785, 1850,