{"id": "364", "question": "how to get antique effect on wood using normal paint", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some steps to achieve an antique effect on wood using normal paint:\n\n1. Apply a basecoat of flat or satin paint to the wood surface ( passage 3). Satin paint is easier to work with, but flat paint will give a more aged look.\n2. Mix paint that is two shades darker than the basecoat with an equal amount of glaze ( passage 3). Once the basecoat is dry, drizzle a crumpled rag or plastic bag in the glaze mixture to achieve an aged appearance.\n3. Layer stain on top of the paint to create a cool effect ( passage 1). This technique involves applying multiple layers of stain and allowing them to dry between coats.\n4. Use a palette knife to spread a mixture of molding paste and a rust or dull color on a canvas, letting it dry before adding additional layers ( passage 2). This can help create an embossed design with an antique finish.\n5. Apply a thin layer of burnt sienna or a similar color to give the wood a vintage look ( passage 3). Dabbing the paint with a paper towel or cloth can help remove excess paint and achieve a more subtle, aged appearance.\n\nOverall, the key to achieving an antique effect on wood using normal paint is to layer different materials and colors to create a weathered and worn appearance.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nStep 1: Identify the materials needed for achieving an antique effect on wood using normal paint.\nStep 2: Explain the process of applying the paint to the wood, including the specific techniques needed.\nStep 3: Provide examples of colors and techniques that can create an antique effect.\nStep 4: Discuss any necessary tools or additional steps for finishing the wood to enhance the antique look.\nStep 5: Summarize the key points for achieving the desired antique effect on wood using normal paint.\nAnswer: Unable to answer based on given passages.\nStep 1: Identify the materials needed for achieving an antique effect on wood using normal paint.\nStep 2: Explain the process of applying the paint to the wood, including the specific techniques needed.\nStep 3: Provide examples of colors and techniques that can create an antique effect.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 45.386, "generation_time_seconds": 30.9264, "selection_time_seconds": 14.4598, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [5501, 3410, 279, 21085, 429, 686, 387, 1483, 311, 4226, 279, 3405, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-4.8488, -0.1827, -0.0284, -2.1271, -2.3035, -3.5916, -0.1026, -0.0209, -0.05, -0.0301, -0.3433, -0.0026, -0.0169, -0.002], "cumulative_logprob": -13.6505, "norm_logp": -0.975, "input_len": 142, "output_tokens": 14}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [840, 19991, 279, 10434, 315, 279, 7579, 1573, 18824, 13, 64547, 894, 5810, 12624, 476, 16772, 24384, 429, 646, 387, 23922, 311, 1855, 458, 46591, 2456, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.6345, -0.0559, -0.042, -5.0589, -1.3707, -0.0173, -0.5818, -2.678, -0.9707, -1.1615, -3.4172, -0.8143, -0.474, -1.4957, -0.254, -0.6168, 0.0, -0.1625, -0.7941, -0.3087, -0.927, -0.6566, -0.2134, -0.0884, -0.0004, -0.0516, -0.0063, -0.0414], "cumulative_logprob": -23.8937, "norm_logp": -0.8533, "input_len": 142, "output_tokens": 28}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [840, 19991, 279, 4494, 315, 56848, 429, 525, 14452, 369, 46591, 6239, 389, 7579, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.6345, -0.0559, -0.042, -1.666, -0.0023, -1.626, -1.6758, -2.3255, -1.1181, -0.0001, -4.433, -0.3217, -0.7446, -0.0005, -0.1278, -0.0871], "cumulative_logprob": -15.8609, "norm_logp": -0.9913, "input_len": 142, "output_tokens": 16}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [8304, 220, 16, 2