{"id": "735", "question": "what is the difference between sms and mms", "gt": "SMS stands for Short Message Service, commonly referred to as a text message, and allows you to send a text message of up to 160 characters to another device. MMS, which stands for Multimedia Messaging Service, is an evolution of SMS. It allows you to send messages that include pictures, video, or audio content to another device. MMS messages can carry a larger size of content up to 300KB. SMS is restricted to sending text-only messages, whereas MMS allows a combination of text, pictures, sounds, and videos.", "pred": "The main difference between SMS and MMS is the type of content that can be sent. SMS is limited to text messages, while MMS can send a variety of multimedia content. Additionally, MMS may have higher bandwidth usage and data limits compared to SMS, and it may have different security and privacy features.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_tot", "raw_output": "The main difference between SMS and MMS is the type of content that can be sent. SMS is limited to text messages, while MMS can send a variety of multimedia content. Additionally, MMS may have higher bandwidth usage and data limits compared to SMS, and it may have different security and privacy features.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nIdentify key differences in functionality, such as the ability to send text messages (SMS) versus multimedia messages (MMS).\nCompare the transmission of text-only messages to the ability to send images, videos, and audio files in MMS.\nNote any differences in bandwidth usage and data limits between SMS and MMS.\nExamine the security and privacy features that may vary between SMS and MMS.\nConsider any limitations or restrictions that may apply to MMS usage, such as storage limits or specific device compatibility.\nDescribe any differences in the user interface or process of sending messages between SMS and MMS.\nAnalyze any specific examples or real-world use cases provided in the passages to illustrate the practical differences between SMS and MMS.\nIdentify any specific examples or real-world use cases provided in the passages to illustrate the practical differences between SMS and MMS.\n\nSMS (Short Message Service) is a text-based messaging service that allows users to send and receive text messages. It is a simple and straightforward way to communicate with others, and it is often used for quick and informal communication.\n\nMMS (Multimedia Messaging Service) is a more advanced messaging service that allows users to send and receive multimedia messages, such as images, videos, and audio files. It is a more complex and versatile way to communicate with others, and it is often used for more formal and detailed communication.\n\nThe main difference between SMS and MMS is the type of content that can be sent. SMS is limited to text messages, while MMS can send a variety of multimedia content. Additionally, MMS may have higher bandwidth usage and data limits compared to SMS, and it may have different security and privacy features.", "time_sec": 52.24, "generation_time_seconds": 30.516, "selection_time_seconds": 21.724, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [27374, 29909, 323, 386, 4826, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-3.8151, -1.4152, -0.0163, -0.0005, -0.0, -2.7135, -0.0051], "cumulative_logprob": -7.9657, "norm_logp": -1.138, "input_len": 141, "output_tokens": 7}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [4418, 279, 2661, 46769, 323, 10542, 894, 11799, 9733, 1948, 29909, 323, 386, 4826, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-4.1722, -0.5293, -0.8323, -0.2966, -3.0032, -0.1607, -1.3486, -3.2398, -1.2298, -0.014, -0.0001, -0.0195, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0338, -0.1026], "cumulative_logprob": -14.9825, "norm_logp": -0.9364, "input_len": 141, "output_tokens": 16}, {"phase": "rollout", "beam": 0, "token_ids": [785, 21085, 1558, 537, 3410, 1995, 911, 279, 6672